 columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 12 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{6}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-1,$ $b=0,$ $c=1$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-1)+0+1=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82493" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e46cb2ea0664f07305e82609808e