"attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 & -2 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 8 & -5 & 19 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -5 & 19 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-3,$ $b=1,$ $c=19$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=(-3)+1+19=17.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82501" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e46cb2ea0664f07305e82609808ebbfd6440e117d12a2911ac18ad02f5cac21"} {"question" "q-82501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "17"] ["math" "19"] ["math" "20"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82501" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "17"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & b & -25 \\\\ a & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT([\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}y-z \\\\ x+y-z \\\\ x+y\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nFirst, let's find the image of ${b}_{1},$ ${b}_{2},$ and ${b}_{3}$ under $T,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 & 0 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 & 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}-{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -1 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & -7 & 3 & -25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 & -25 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 2 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=3,$ $c=7$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+3+7=10.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82497" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e46cb2ea0664f07305e82609808ebbfd6440e117d12a2911ac18ad02f5cac21"} {"question" "q-82497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82497" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "10"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The basis $B$ of ${R}^{3}$ and the matrix $[\\,T\\,]_{B}$ of the linear transformation $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ relative to $B$ are given by\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}a & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & b & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n$$\nT({b}_{1})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,T({b}_{3})=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}.$ The $B$-matrix for $T$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $B$, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {b}_{3} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $B$-matrix for $T:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 1 & -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n[\\,T\\,]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{2} & -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=\\frac{1}{2},$ $c=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c=0+\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2})=-1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3e46cb2ea0664f07305e82609808ebbfd6440e117d12a2911ac18ad02f5cac21"} {"question" "q-82483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-1"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "q-82483" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/state.json" "file_sha256" "d46d37e4b5fc29dfdc41d921c01c53f0ee1472dab43dafbc6f43662a5f56083e"} {"question" "e-5514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/ce5c4a3b67cdbcb3fd7a808d201cc5dd0247408788957487d6b6898622cd0ff8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-5514.json" "file_sha256" "100de149c8f5b516c648d20899807881c5b003598dcb8f3030672a9898c11a53"} {"question" "e-5514" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The image of $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T(x)]_{B} & =[T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-5514.json" "file_sha256" "100de149c8f5b516c648d20899807881c5b003598dcb8f3030672a9898c11a53"} {"question" "e-7249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ The matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/e07cc9382ff64be2d3ad03974dee5e61f814457f06dbe4e755a72e69efc7608c.png)\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({b}_{1}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{3}, \\\\ T({b}_{2}) & ={b}_{1}+{b}_{2}, \\\\ T({b}_{3}) & ={b}_{2}+{b}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-7249.json" "file_sha256" "f1ec66283d123acab41053868a7e077dbb0bc4e8a00214cb310045143b3557e6"} {"question" "e-7249" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/9b7de12d5e69074e64281b8060088e458c04007ed48f7640e85619898f96dad7.png)\n\nNow, the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix  whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ Therefore, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/2dd5d507303f356fcd3632c8dade2e8a656169de1118b4443bd651cca45d6660.png)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=1,$ $c=0$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+0 = 2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-7249.json" "file_sha256" "f1ec66283d123acab41053868a7e077dbb0bc4e8a00214cb310045143b3557e6"} {"question" "e-5513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/ec340f6251c31a0445c5e3db9e23488e783e36eb2cc2e3f85d83993a3ac87f79.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-5513.json" "file_sha256" "465c9d4e4b7e3cf67279fb9da1ee0e9a52dfe102d8b24603b2df91f657b57eea"} {"question" "e-5513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}.$ The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/5228bf96b160625889e18bd57146d6ce092f48859f5a96bd77e4832a0fc6362f.png)\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}-{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/32aab8b678ff195f1ce2edf43bd1565890328a8926aa22c31b1850936e54a927.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-5513.json" "file_sha256" "465c9d4e4b7e3cf67279fb9da1ee0e9a52dfe102d8b24603b2df91f657b57eea"} {"question" "e-5515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are given by\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/6efd50facaf6c5a4d4b536eaa194bbfcf240cf30804bb13a7a6ac479adf6d864.png)\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that  \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/1d585e7b839d1e569693d7489c23e47761e2f96bdf114ee96f4b287aa68d7fa4.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-5515.json" "file_sha256" "e439e2f33d1cd9c15a0d77b4e859d8b2b6cfd6a7f1ec2bc632d7b12d26b23373"} {"question" "e-5515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}.$ The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1),$ $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2),$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/318cfa577ad284a0b606eb58232ee0276deedcbd446357e7f5b4c690b46b2208.png)\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/assets/5181a3a65f33553fad4c1ebf5ac81e9c2e3957576ac0501b953afcbf8c09349d.png)\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ $c=-2$ and \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-2)+(-2) = -3.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14066994/example-5515.json" "file_sha256" "e439e2f33d1cd9c15a0d77b4e859d8b2b6cfd6a7f1ec2bc632d7b12d26b23373"}] "usage" {"q-38087" [] "e-5513" [] "e-5515" [] "e-7249" [] "e-5514" [] "q-38188" []} "basis" 2221 "retractions" [[17592186125035 :question/problem "Let $B = {b_{1}, b_{2}}$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T(x)]_{B}$, given that\n\n$[T]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $[x]_{B} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186125038 :question/problem "Let $B$ be a basis of $R^{2}$ and let $T: R^{2} → R^{2}$ be a linear transformation. Find $[T]_{B}$, given that\n\n$T(b_{1}) = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}$, $T(b_{2}) = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and $B = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}$."] [17592186124267 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{x})]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124267 :question/worked-solution "The image of $[\\mathbf{x}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n[T(x)]_{B} &= [T]_{B}[x]_{B} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-7 \\\\ 16\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124301 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$. The matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}:\\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ is given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & c \\\\ 0 & b & 1 \\\\ a & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFind the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that\n\n$T(b_{1})|= b_{1} + b_{3},; T(b_{2})|= b_{1} + b_{2},; T(b_{3})|= b_{2} + b_{3}$."] [17592186124301 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the coordinates of the images relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$. Therefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=1$, $b=1$, $c=0$ and\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+1+0 = 2\n$$"] [17592186124266 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and let $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^2 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^2$ be a linear transformation. Find $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$, given that\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124266 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$. The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$ and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; b_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & T(b_{1})\\end{vmatrix}T(b_{2}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -4 & -7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=\\frac{1}{2}R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} - R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & \\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{2} & 5 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186124268 :question/problem "The basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and the matrix $[\\,\\mathbf{T}\\,]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}: \\mathbb{R}^3 \\rightarrow \\mathbb{R}^3$ relative to $\\mathcal{B}$ are given by\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\,a+b+c,\\,$ given that\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-9 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124268 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\}$. The $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nTo find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_1)$, $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_2)$, and $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{b}_3)$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, we consider the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; b_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}b_{2} & b_{3} & T(b_{1}) & T(b_{2})\\end{vmatrix}T(b_{3}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nNow, we reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination. Then, on the right-hand side, we will obtain the $\\mathcal{B}$-matrix for $\\mathbf{T}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 4 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}R_{3} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 0 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 3R_{3} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 0 & -9 & 7 & 10 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{1}:=R_{1} + 3R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, $a=1$, $b=-2$, $c=-2$ and\n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+(-2)+(-2) = -3\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186123233 17592186123234 17592186123235 17592186123236 17592186123237 17592186123251 17592186123252 17592186123253 17592186123254 17592186123255] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123232 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123232 :answer-field/id] [17592186123232 :answer-field/key] [17592186123232 :answer-field/type] [17592186123250 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123250 :answer-field/id] [17592186123250 :answer-field/key] [17592186123250 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
